Z||000014||artherd||08-14-2001||04:00 PM||||Hrm, seems odd...<P>if the friction surface of a centreforce gets hot enough it will glaze like any other clutch.  However it's gotta get exceptionally hot.  And I can't imagine how the metal side has gotten slipry, unless it somehow has been 'glazed' too, but I've never seen that occur (dosen't mean it's impossible though.)<P>How much bhp are we talking about here? why 4th, are tyres limiting factor in every other gear (!!!)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RDosdorian:<BR><B>  I hit 4th and it just slips.  I am told it is because of the horsepower the motor is producing at that point.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000015||California Kid||08-14-2001||07:52 PM||||The only clutch, from 4 Top Manufacturers, found to hold up in my car is QuarterMaster. All other clutches tried either slipped in the high gears or were just destroyed in under 1,000 miles (I mean torn apart!). <P>Setup consists of a Titanium ClutchPak Housing, 1 7.5 inch Clutch Disc faced both sides, 1 7.5 toothed 1/8 inch thick Steel Floater Ring (outer teeth engage into titanium housing), 1 7.5 inch Clutch Disc faced both sides, and Pressure Plate (centrifugal-more rpm, more force) bolted to titanium housing-to flywheel.<P>Note: Flywheel has to be machined for this setup, and insert plate made for clutch bearing surface.<P>Not cheap, but holds the power.<P>If anyone can run 12's with another setup, please post, and let us know what kind of mileage you're getting out of the clutch disk/or disk's. The QuarterMaster Setup above get's about 10,000 very hard street miles before the disk's have to be replaced. At the first sign of clutch slip, you must replace disk's ASAP, it doesn't take to much slipping to warp the insert plate (heat builds up real quick, just think about the forces and horsepower).<P>You could get more miles out of them if you took it easy most of the time, but anyone who's driven a V8 know's it's hard to keep your foot out of it.<P>The only other solution for a manual trans that I can think of would require some "heavy machine work". Meaning surgery on the Getrag bellhousing (removing) and adapting a larger V8 Bellhousing onto it (larger diameter clutch could be used then).<P>Tenths of a second come at a high price once you're in the very low 13's.<P>One other point I should make about the QuarterMaster Clutch, there are no springs in the hub (it's solid), and you shouldn't slip the clutch (either in or out) as once you glaze it both disk's are shot. As a result, you are loading up the trans, CV joints and half shafts with an incredible about of force. If you start using really sticky Drag Slicks you're probably going to split the trans in half or blow apart the drivers side inboard CV joint (which shears the half shaft out at the same time).<P>It's a real sweeeettt setup once you realize what the limitations are. If you think you can pull 4 to 5 grand hole shots (with good radial street tires) on drive pavement, you're going to be calling for a flatbed tow truck. Remember, Mid engine high horse power fiero's don't like it!!! You have to know how and when to feed this horse. I've even thought of going with some harder compound street tires (less grip), to reduce the forces on the trans and half shafts (let the tires spin alittle).<P>Just thought I'd share some of my experiences with you guys.<BR>Tom<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||RDosdorian||08-14-2001||08:24 PM||||California Kid...I would like to contact you directly regarding these clutch issues.  By the sounds of it, you have gone through exactly what I am going through.  Could you e-mail me directly (mrdetail@juno.com) so I can ask some questions.  Maybe leave your number and a good time for me to call and I will call you.  <P>Thanks,<P>Ray<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.goldengatefieros.org/RDosdorian_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||lildevil||08-14-2001||09:32 PM||||Saw the topic and thought I add my two cents. Please remember I am not trying to start any thing but i jus want to state some observations.  Ok first off my car has the 3.8 SC in it and it has so far run a best of 13.311 on street tires on a warm day.  And the tires slip. Getting it to a 13.1 or 13.0 does seem that far off with the current set up. Ok now heres what i noticed.  And i hope noone takes this the wrong way but its jus an observation.  Those of you with the V-8's seem to have spent a small fortune on the conversions and in the tuning the motors. Where as with the 3.8 I spent 4000 dollars on the conversion and motor and trans. Its a auto also.  I drive the car EVERYDAY and race it on the weekends.   And with a NOS kit I would be in the low twelves for sure.  <BR>With an intercooler and some heads and other stuff who knows.... But my questions is...is the 3.8 the most cost effective of the engine conversions?  Most Reliable?<P>Jus my 2 cents...hope this didn't ruffle any feathers.<P>Keep those times coming in...<P><P>------------------<BR>Earl Sessions<BR>1985 Fiero w/ 1998 Supercharged 3.8 V-6<BR>Best ET: 13.311 @100.75 mph<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000018||California Kid||08-14-2001||10:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lildevil:<BR><B>for sure.  <BR>But my questions is...is the 3.8 the most cost effective of the engine conversions?  Most Reliable?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>No flame intended with this reply!!!<P>Speed cost money pure and simple. It also uses up engines and drivetrains much faster. The small block V8 Chevy has an unbeatable track record for durability and should also mention cost, parts availability etc, etc. You might also want check out the horsepower that they will produce on the "bottle" and they are still very sound engines (meaning without the bottle they'll even last allot longer). Putting mine on the bottle would more than likely put it into the 9's, but in order to get there I'm would be needing probably some other clutch setup/trans/& half shafts. The car is fast enough already, with very good engine durability.<P>The 3800 in my mind hasn't proved itself 100% yet when it's "pumped up".  You may be the first one to venture into these waters. Big question is how long is it going to last when you it into the 12 second bracket ???<BR>I really hope that shortly down the road you'll be able to comment on this with very surprising results, like no failures after about 50 runs. It would be very good information for the rest of us when considering our next project.<P>Again, no flame intended, you have a real special car and I wish you the best.<BR>Tom<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||KRMFiero||08-14-2001||10:44 PM||||I hope i can get Low 14's with my 4.5/440T   maybe even a high 13...  ahh thats next year till it can make it to track  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red) Caddy 4.5 Project<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>       <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> <BR>AOL: Physcokyle||||reg||1
Z||000020||NOS3800||08-14-2001||10:55 PM||||I think what LilDevil is getting at, and I agree is that the v-8 guys are having alot of issues with trannies, not the blocks. It has always been an issue that the manual trannies just can't get enough clutch. With the 3800 SC motor, Devil has a smaller pulley and reprogrammed ECM, thats it and he is running low 13's. <BR>This is where it gets interesting, the GrandPrix list has guys with DAILY drivers with even smaller pulleys, shift kits, bored throttle bodies, FPR, and throw in NOS for giggles. WITHOUT the NOS their GP's are running 12's-low 13's. Thats with FWD and a 3600 lb car! Check out the latest issue of HP Pontiac. When Devil gets around to getting a smaller pulley, and maybe a shift kit he won't need NOS to run 12's. It will be reliable, and still a daily driver! Just my two cents, not a flame. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Pontiaddict||08-14-2001||11:46 PM||||Yes, but you're comparing automatics to manual transmissions. Take a 12 second 3800, mate it to a manual transaxle, and see if you have the same problems the v8 guys are having.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||cardealer||08-14-2001||11:47 PM||||lildevil -<BR>no offense taken, and your are more than likely correct on your obversations with v8 vs the 3800sc setups. but there are pros and cons on both sides. if you have to rebuild well good luck on the 3800sc getting parts as plentyful and cheap as the the v8's. i love my v8 fiero, but i will never build another. i have less than $5000.00 including the car invested in my entire setup. and the potenial of upgrading for less than $1000.00 more could result in a mid to a low 11.00 street car. it would be impossible for me to make a mid 11.00 fiero with only $6000.00 and a 3800sc. i would love to have a 3800sc fiero (with a/c!!!) for the dependablity and just an overall fun car. the reponse i get from others that see the car, i think would not be as great with a 3800sc as opposed to the n2o v8 fiero. there is a couple of parking lots in my city that all the hotrodders hang out on saturday nights, the first time i took my car out there everybody (close to 70 people) came swarming around my car. and these guys with blown v8s and tubbed rearends were really impressed it really made me feel that all the work i did was all worth it. but when the damn thing overheats.... i want roll down down the window just a little bit and put $10.00 of gas through the window (inside the car) and throw a match inside and walk away... the end... ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Jimmy||08-15-2001||12:39 AM||||Wow, Cardealer, send me a description of your cooling system and maybe I can help you out.  I have had NO PROBLEMS with my V8 cooling setup and that includes a couple hours of rush hour traffic with a heat index of 110 degrees.  <P>As to 3.8 SC with over 300 hp/ftbls and clutches, it would have the same problems as a V8 Fiero as both motors are tourque monsters and they use the same tranny/clutch.  I am waiting on feedback from a local member that is trying out a four puck kevlar cluch disk.  He is still breaking it in but so far he really likes it.<P>Yes my clutch setup(centerforce dual friction, not Archies) will slip slightly every once in awhile but it seems to do it less and less(2000 miles on setup, hard driving).  I would also like to point out that my V8 setup has not been track tested yet so I won't comment on what I think I might run.  Can't wait to get it to the track however!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Jim<BR>87 GT<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8||||reg||1
Z||000024||California Kid||08-15-2001||12:42 AM||||cardealer-<P>Tony, know you're running a 5 core radiator and two fans, but what degree thermal switch are you running, and are you running a thermostat ??<P>My L98 (400 hp) runs cooler than my V6, 4 core radiator, 165 thermal switch, stock fan, and restrictor flange at water inlet on engine (20% reduction in opening).  Initial engine timing set at 8 degree advance. I'm running fuel injection, but shouldn't make a difference unless you're running pretty lean on the gas with the carb. ||||reg||5
Z||000025||California Kid||08-15-2001||01:29 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cardealer:<BR><B><BR>The reponse i get from others that see the car, i think would not be as great with a 3800sc as opposed to the n2o v8 fiero. there is a couple of parking lots in my city that all the hotrodders hang out on saturday nights, the first time i took my car out there everybody (close to 70 people) came swarming around my car. and these guys with blown v8s and tubbed rearends were really impressed it really made me feel that all the work i did was all worth it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cardealer makes an excellent point here, and I'm not slamming any other conversions, but the V8's make everybody think "No Way!!!", Holy S***!!!, whatever...typical responses. On a side note, I can enter 95% of the really good Hot Rod shows here in the Detroit area and walk away with a trophy. It's not that the trophy itself means much, but it does make you feel better about your toy investment. The payback for me is just watching the look on people faces as they look intently at the car, get to the engine bay and are stumped!!! Hot Rod Shows are where these cars really come alive.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BEST_IN_CLASS_post.jpg">  <BR>Trophy above from All Pontiac Show early this year.<P>It also took 3rd Place at the big GM TECH CTR Show for 1971 to Current All Makes, All Models Modified. Really stiff competiton with full mechanical check. Just to mention two shows.<P>Wouldn't have happened with any V6, didn't want to say that, I like your cars as much as you do, but it's the truth.<P><p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||cardealer||08-15-2001||10:11 AM||||my over heating deal...<BR>the car will run pretty cool around town, and at idle all day long. i have a 180 degree thermo in there now, but as soon as i start getting on it or make a few shots of the n2o it go's around 210 degrees and wont come back down. at the track, forget it!!! took almost all day and to make 6 runs. after i shut it down it will go to 250 degrees and i start losing my coolant. i have tried a 160 degree same thing. i purchased a 195 dregree last night, i think i am going to try it out.(?)i am using the following cooling set up:<BR>5 core rad<BR>stewart high volume water pump<BR>two electric fans- 1 push, 1 pull<BR>fans run on toggle switch<BR>180 degree thermo<BR>2365 curse words<BR>3621 prayers<BR>(at last obversation none seem to be working)||||reg||8
Z||000027||California Kid||08-15-2001||10:26 AM||||Cardealer-<BR>It is possible that the thermostat is restricting the flow too much. I would try making a restrictor plate to go in place off the thermostat. A thin piece (1/16" inch copper plate and gasket each side) of metal and make the hole in it about 1/4" smaller evenly around the opening in your intake manifold. If you can't find copper, use stainless steel (grade 409).<P>Pump I'm using is the standard Corvette short shaft.<P>There is another possibility for the high temps you may want to investigate. You should be adding more gas (seperate squirter)when you hit the bottle. If you don't, I've heard that engine temperature will go through the roof and pretty soon you're burning through the top of the pistons. Get ahold of some articles or ask some of the hot rod boy's running hot bottle setups.<BR>Hope this helps.<p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||Hartz||08-15-2001||11:02 AM||||I can attest to California Kid's statement regarding V8s.  My Turbo V6 is SO clean.  There is hardly a visible flaw on it, I've spent YEARs working on it, and whether or not the air dam and body kit are to your tastes or not, it is obvious that a lot of work has gone into this car.  That being said, if I take that car out, I get very few comments.  If I take my V8 car out, which is very much a cosmetic "work in progress" I need to plan on two hours of standing around answering questions.  <P>I am sure this is because most are not knowledgeable about Fieros, but saying you have a V6 sounds normal, whereas even my grandmother knows a V8 isn't supposed to be back there!<P>I think it is also interesting that the majority of people who do NOT like the V8 conversions are what I would call "Fiero People," in that they ARE knowledgable about the cars.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||8
Z||000029||cardealer||08-15-2001||11:34 AM||||cal kid,<BR>i forgot to mention a few things, i tried using water restrictors bought the whole set, tried all of them. i was getting to much water flow. my bottom pulley (crankshaft) is the same size as an alternator pulley, so therefore it is turning the pump faster than normal. plus here in austin we have be living in 100 plus degree weather all summer. maybe i should try a regular water pump or a csi??? ||||reg||1
Z||000030||California Kid||08-15-2001||11:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>Cardealer-<BR>edited while you were posting your last message.<P>There is another possibility for the high temps you may want to investigate. You should be adding more gas (seperate squirter)when you hit the bottle. If you don't, I've heard that engine temperature will go through the roof and pretty soon you're burning through the top of the pistons. Get ahold of some articles or ask some of the hot rod boy's running hot bottle setups.<BR>Hope this helps.<P>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 08-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000031||BBBAD GT||08-15-2001||08:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JayUSA77:<BR><B>i going for a 10.99 with a 99LS1 with headers, air itake, power pully, MSD, and 150 shot of nitrous. around 550 HP on the bottle.... what do you guys think. 10.99? and if i dont get 10.9 of that im putting a cam and ported heads.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> i will hit 10.99 <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>j<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Good luck with the 10.99.  I'm not putting you down, I think everybody needs a goal, but I really don't think 4 bolt ons (including N2O) will get you threre, even in a 2900lb Fiero.  If it was that easy, everybody would be doing that with their LT1/LS1 and turning 11.30's with the heavier weight.  Any car that goes 10.99 needs more work than just a big motor. <P>Like I said, good luck and keep me posted when do you run the number.<P><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">         <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale *SOLD*</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Jimmy||08-15-2001||10:57 PM||||Cardealer this is the setup I have with my carburated V8 350.  Custom built 4-core radiator(bolt in replacement of stock rad), CSI waterpump 35-40GPH, stock cooling fan.  For a thermostat I can run 160 or just a restictor(thermostat with the "guts" punched out).  I also ran w/o a thermostat but the engine would never get over 160 degrees.  I also run my rad fan at all times.  I would say 1500 of the 2000 miles I have on this car was on VERY hot conditions for weather.  I also do not have any type of hood scoops installed to aid with cooling.<P>Problems you may have: Are you sure your push pull fans(I would have no idea as I havn't seen it) are the best setup?  Have you tried just using the stock fan?  How about your cooling tubes?  Are they crushed or twisted?  I would try w/o a thermostat and see if it gets better.  Then work your way up from there.  I also agree about running the nitrous, make sure the car isn't running lean or you could be in trouble.<P>One thing I have noticed about my CSI pump is that when your car does seem to get hot(happened once when I was low on coolant), just slow down or let it idle a bit, it will cool right down in less than a minute.  I am very happy with my CSI.<P>Jim<BR>87 GT<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8||||reg||1
Z||000033||Archie||08-16-2001||12:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jimmy:<BR><B>  I am very happy with my CSI.<P>Jim<BR>87 GT<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I totally agree. I've been testing the CSI extensively on my "beater" & I can't believe how well it works.<P>I'm using my 4 core rad. and a LM1 engine with TPI & a 180* 'stat. & no hood venting etc. I have never had it over 190*. I did have to hook up the rad fan to the TPI ECM, so that it would not be running all the time. Now the ECM turns the fan on and off as needed. When I had the fan running all the time, the engine wouldn't get warm enough to go into "Closed Loop".<P>I have exactly the same engine setup in my Finale but it has the Belt W/P Drive and my opinion at this time is that the CSI works just as good if not better.<P>Right now I'm looking for a couple of "Test" type Water Temp gages. (the ones that have a wire lookin' probe that you can insert into the water stream at a hose connection) I want several of them all the same style so I can put them in various places in the cooling system for testing. At some point I want to determine WHICH is BETTER between the CSI and my W/P drive. <P>If anyone knows where I can look for the temp gages, pease let me know.<P>Archie<P>||||reg||14
Z||000034||86SE||08-16-2001||02:03 AM||||With 550 HP you could push a 3000lb Fiero into 10's if you dont have huge 18" wheels and something to keep the front end on the ground.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Jimmy||08-16-2001||02:36 AM||||Yeah Archie I agree about not running the fan all the time.  I am going to stop running my fan "all the time" soon(have to install the sensor for the fan) but since I am using a carb cooler temps are ok for now.<P>Jim<BR>87 Gt<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8||||reg||1
Z||000036||artherd||08-16-2001||03:37 AM||||HI Cardealer, I belive your water pump may be cavitating!<P>An alternator size pully will spin very fast, depending on your main crank pully size, up to around 18-24,000rpm.  This is at least twice as fast as the water pump should be spinning.<P>(cavitating occurs when any impeller spins too fast in a water solution.  Air actually forms from abuse on the water, and you can imagine the effect of a perminant air bubble around your impeller.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cardealer:<BR><B>cal kid,<BR>i forgot to mention a few things, i tried using water restrictors bought the whole set, tried all of them. i was getting to much water flow. my bottom pulley (crankshaft) is the same size as an alternator pulley, so therefore it is turning the pump faster than normal. plus here in austin we have be living in 100 plus degree weather all summer. maybe i should try a regular water pump or a csi??? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000037||pontiacs4me||08-16-2001||04:17 AM||||Im going to throw in a few words about the v6 versus v8 discussion earlier in the topic.  A friend of mine has a L67 3800 supercharged in his 1991 lumina ( <A HREF="http://www.luminasupercharged.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.luminasupercharged.com</A>  ) he is running the stock computer with a smaller pulley, and has run a best of 13.80 at 99.7 with no nitrous.  this weighing 3550 lbs.  Ad the 75 shot of nitrous and he pulled off a 12.87 at 111.  He has made probably 50-60 passes with this engine, and hasnt had one bit of trouble.  Right now he is working on a custom phenolic blower spacer, and external blower cooling system for his setup.  Along with polishing his supercharger to a high gloss shine.  (I dont think this will make it any faster though, but it will look very nice)  After he ads underdrive pulleys for the entire engine, and gets the rest of his cold air setup done, he should be able to hit 12.50.  Now imagine this in a car that weighs 700 lbs less.  BTW my car weighs 2850, so this is 700 lbs.  Thats the equivalent of adding 70 more horsepower to the engine.  When he added a 75 shot of nitrous he picked up just over 9/10ths of a second.  So by adding 70 more horsepower lets say youll pick up .50 more, there you go 12 flat.  In a v6 no less, that still gets 20 mpg in town and 25 on the highway.  And when you show the guys at the street races whats under the deck, they see a v6, theyre like holy sh!t, my viper just got its ass kicked by a fiero with a v6.  Its just more satisfying than having a v8.  Yes v8's are much more capable of making more power, personally I believe the L67's are more reliable.  Besides the fact of getting better gas mileage, where our government finds it necessary to make us pay in upwards of 2 dollars a gallon in the summer for premium gasoline.  <BR>In conclusion, people have the power to choose to drive whatever they want.  If every fiero on the road had a v6 the world would be even more boring than it is now.  Same goes if every fiero had a v8 or four cylinder.  people make their cars unique to keep things interesting.  Personally I find it more gratifying to have a v6 that will kill camaros with theyre small blocks.  SBC's are a dime a dozen.  Go to any drag strip in the country, probably 75% of the cars there have chevy small blocks in them, why? because they are cheap to build.  When you blow it up (which happens primarialy because they arent very well designed engines to start with) you can build another one for pennies on the dollar compared to a real engine like a pontiac 455, and get back out there racing and having fun, the next weekend.  Instead of working overtime for 8 weekends in a row trying to save up the money to fix the pontiac (which most likely wouldnt of happened in the first place, because they were well designed engines in the beginning)<BR>Sorry SBC guys, had to do it.  Flame on!||||reg||1
Z||000038||Archie||08-16-2001||07:31 AM||||Cardealer..... take another look at this.<P>If you crankshaft pulley is smaller than the W/P pulley then your W/P is running SLOWER than the crank.<P>If you have a alternator size pulley on the crank and a normal size pulley on the W/P, then the W/P is only running like 25% of the crank speed.<P>It's running way too slow.<P>Archie<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cardealer:<BR><B>cal kid,<BR> my bottom pulley (crankshaft) is the same size as an alternator pulley, so therefore it is turning the pump faster than normal. plus here in austin we have be living in 100 plus degree weather all summer. maybe i should try a regular water pump or a csi??? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||4
Z||000039||cardealer||08-16-2001||02:19 PM||||archie wins !!!!!<BR>after about two hours of intense (no beer involed)looking at my setup i came the same  conclusion yesterday. i run only 1 belt and i noticed sometimes while the car is at idle after driving awhile the alt light comes on. so now i need to come up with another pulley setup. what size of crank pulley are you v8 guys running?||||reg||1
